<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heo Hwang-ok</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heo Hwang-ok is the legendary queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suro-of-geumgwan-gaya">King Suro</a>, founder of Geumgwan Gaya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a> says she arrived by sea in 48 CE from a distant land called Ayuta, which many in modern times identify with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gimhae">Gimhae</a> Kim and Heo clans trace their descent from her, and her reputed tomb is in Gimhae.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Her story comes from the Garakguk-gi, the Record of Garak Kingdom, a work now lost that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a> cites. It makes her a princess of Ayuta, which the surviving records call only a distant country. According to the legend, the Heavenly Lord appeared in her parents' dreams and told them to send her to Suro, who had been chosen as king of Kaya and had not yet found a queen. Her father sent her, and she reached Kaya after two months at sea.</p><p>Suro had refused the maidens his courtiers proposed, saying that the Heavens would choose his wife. His officer Yuch'ŏn-gan met a vessel with a red sail and a red flag off the island of Mangsan-do and escorted it to shore. The princess would not go to the palace with strangers, so the king had a tent pitched on a hill nearby, where she arrived with the courtiers Sin Po and Cho Kwang, their wives and twenty slaves bearing gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, jewels and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. She told the king she was 16 years old.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Genealogical tradition gives the couple 12 children. At her request two of them bore her surname, and the Gimhae Heo trace their origin to them; the Gimhae Kim and the Yi clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incheon">Incheon</a> trace theirs to eight of the sons. More than six million present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> are said to descend from her.</p><p>Near the grave held to be hers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gimhae">Gimhae</a> stands a pagoda of a rough form unlike any other in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, traditionally said to have come on her ship. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a> records that King Jilji built a temple, Wanghusa, in 452 where the couple married; modern scholars read it as an ancestral shrine, since no other record shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in 5th-century Kaya. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the South Korean First Lady Kim Jung-sook laid the foundation stone for the expansion of a memorial to her on 6 November 2018.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Most academics reject the story. It rests on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a> alone: the older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a> does not mention her, and scholars believe that its author, the Buddhist monk Il-yeon, exaggerated to create familiarity with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. The same source has Suro live to 157 years and turn into an eagle and a hawk. Proposed homelands include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>, by phonetic similarity though it lies far inland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a>, founded only in 1350, and, in the view of Hwa-seob Song, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> or an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> nation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heo Hwang-ok” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founding legend tells of King Suro hatching from a golden egg in 42 CE and marrying <b>Heo Hwang-ok</b>, a princess said to have sailed from a distant land called Ayuta.</blockquote><a href="/state/geumgwan-gaya">Geumgwan Gaya</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gimhae">Gimhae</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.23,128.88" aria-label="Gimhae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7813 890 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Heo Hwang-ok"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8263 1160h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8263 1160h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=35.23,128.88">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
